The tavern hall is a rectangular space measuring thirty feet from north to south and twenty feet east‑west, with a wooden floor that groans under footfall. The front door sits centered on the east wall; it is a single plank door six feet wide by seven feet high, hinged to its southern side. Opposite, the back door centers the west wall, five feet wide by seven feet tall and equipped with a wooden latch.

At the middle of the north wall lies the hearth: an eight‑foot stone mantel rising four feet above floor level. A large oak barrel—three feet tall and two feet in diameter—is perched on its sill just east of the hearth; the barrel’s lid rests upon the mantel.

Along the south wall runs a polished oak counter eighteen feet long by four feet wide, one foot above the floor. The far eastern end of this counter holds a high bar stool three feet tall with a seat width of two feet. Above the counter, a wooden shelf—eighteen feet long and two feet high, positioned four feet from the floor—holds crystal cups.

Two rows of tables occupy the hall. Three polished oak tables sit in the front row on the north side: each is four by four feet, placed six feet from the north wall and spaced three feet apart. Two larger tables form the back row on the south side; each measures five by five feet, positioned two feet from the counter and spaced three feet apart. Directly behind the front‑row tables sits a “back table,” 4½ by 4½ feet, eight feet from the north wall.

Each of these six tables carries a lantern mounted on a wooden post three feet tall at its center, providing persistent light across the hall. All objects are fixed in place; no two overlap, and free passage exists between front and back doors through the rows of tables and counter. The layout preserves the medieval tavern atmosphere with polished oak furnishings, a stone hearth, and lantern illumination.
